WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.019
- Some people see the thing that they want

00:00:03.019 --> 00:00:04.785
and some people see the thing

00:00:04.785 --> 00:00:07.456
that prevents them from getting
the thing that they want.

00:00:07.456 --> 00:00:08.992
It's as if an entire generation

00:00:08.992 --> 00:00:10.384
is standing at the foot of a mountain,

00:00:10.384 --> 00:00:12.974
they know exactly what they
want, they can see the summit,

00:00:12.974 --> 00:00:14.310
what they can't see is the mountain.

00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:16.077
People put Harley Davidson
logos on their body

00:00:16.077 --> 00:00:18.577
to say something about who
they are, corporate logo.

00:00:18.577 --> 00:00:21.635
Ain't not Proctor and Gambles
tattooed on anybody's arm.

00:00:21.635 --> 00:00:23.844
Passion is the feeling you have

00:00:23.844 --> 00:00:26.764
that you would probably do this for free,

00:00:26.764 --> 00:00:29.556
you know, and you can't believe
somebody pays you to do it.

00:00:29.556 --> 00:00:31.809
- He's an author, speaker, and consultant

00:00:31.809 --> 00:00:34.177
who writes on leadership and management.

00:00:34.177 --> 00:00:36.585
He joined the Rand Corporation in 2010

00:00:36.585 --> 00:00:39.866
where he advises our military
innovation and planning.

00:00:39.866 --> 00:00:41.462
He's known for popularizing the concepts

00:00:41.462 --> 00:00:44.319
of the golden circle,
and to start with why.

00:00:44.319 --> 00:00:46.239
He's Simon Sinek, and here's my take

00:00:46.239 --> 00:00:48.138
on his top 10 rules for success.

00:00:48.138 --> 00:00:49.749
Rule number six is my personal favorite,

00:00:49.749 --> 00:00:51.591
and make sure to stick
around all the way to the end

00:00:51.591 --> 00:00:53.473
for some special bonus
clips, and as always,

00:00:53.473 --> 00:00:54.891
if Simon says something that really,

00:00:54.891 --> 00:00:56.788
really resonates with you, please leave it

00:00:56.788 --> 00:00:58.424
in the comments below
and put quotes around it

00:00:58.424 --> 00:01:00.743
so other people can be inspired as well.

00:01:00.743 --> 00:01:03.807
(electronic whooshing)

00:01:03.807 --> 00:01:07.474
(upbeat instrumental music)

00:01:13.487 --> 00:01:15.385
Let me tell you a story.

00:01:15.385 --> 00:01:17.416
So a friend of mine and I,

00:01:17.416 --> 00:01:19.681
we went for a run in Central Park.

00:01:19.681 --> 00:01:21.895
The Road Runner's Organization,

00:01:21.895 --> 00:01:24.392
on the weekends they host races.

00:01:24.392 --> 00:01:26.431
And it's very common,
at the end of the race

00:01:26.431 --> 00:01:29.009
they'll have a sponsor who
will give away something,

00:01:29.009 --> 00:01:30.979
apples or bagels, or something.

00:01:30.979 --> 00:01:32.890
And on this particular day when we got to

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:36.144
the end of the run there
were some free bagels,

00:01:36.144 --> 00:01:38.923
and they had picnic tables set up,

00:01:38.923 --> 00:01:41.380
and on one side was a group of volunteers.

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:43.699
On the table were boxes of bagels,

00:01:43.699 --> 00:01:46.835
and on the other side was
a long line of runners

00:01:46.835 --> 00:01:49.398
waiting to get their free bagel.

00:01:49.398 --> 00:01:51.811
So I said to my friend,
"Let's get a bagel."

00:01:51.811 --> 00:01:55.831
And he looked at me and said,
"Nah, that line's too long."

00:01:55.831 --> 00:01:57.914
And I said, "Free bagel."

00:01:59.384 --> 00:02:02.750
And he said, "I don't
want to wait in line."

00:02:02.750 --> 00:02:05.167
And I was like, "Free bagel."

00:02:06.092 --> 00:02:07.641
(audience laughing)

00:02:07.641 --> 00:02:10.211
And he said, "Nah, it's too long!"

00:02:10.211 --> 00:02:11.908
And that's when I realized that

00:02:11.908 --> 00:02:13.840
there's two ways to see the world.

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:16.403
Some people see the thing that they want,

00:02:16.403 --> 00:02:19.540
and some people see the
thing that prevents them

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:21.708
from getting the thing that they want.

00:02:21.708 --> 00:02:23.629
I could only see the bagels.

00:02:23.629 --> 00:02:25.230
He could only see the line.

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:27.250
(audience laughing)

00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:30.598
And so I walked up to the line,

00:02:30.598 --> 00:02:33.181
I leaned in between two people,

00:02:34.350 --> 00:02:38.176
put my hand in the box,
and pulled out two bagels.

00:02:38.176 --> 00:02:41.213
And no one got mad at
me, because the rule is

00:02:41.213 --> 00:02:43.555
you can go after whatever you want,

00:02:43.555 --> 00:02:45.661
you just cannot deny anyone else

00:02:45.661 --> 00:02:48.312
to go after whatever they want.

00:02:48.312 --> 00:02:50.302
Now I had to sacrifice choice,

00:02:50.302 --> 00:02:51.598
I didn't get to choose which bagel I got,

00:02:51.598 --> 00:02:53.427
I got whatever I pulled out,

00:02:53.427 --> 00:02:55.965
but I didn't have to wait in line.

00:02:55.965 --> 00:02:58.190
So the point is, you don't
have to wait in line.

00:02:58.190 --> 00:03:00.787
You don't have to do it the
way everybody else has done it.

00:03:00.787 --> 00:03:04.235
You can do it your way,
you can break the rules,

00:03:04.235 --> 00:03:05.571
you just can't get in the way of

00:03:05.571 --> 00:03:07.569
somebody else getting what they want.

00:03:07.569 --> 00:03:09.361
That's rule number one.

00:03:09.361 --> 00:03:11.344
Performing under
pressure, whether it's me,

00:03:11.344 --> 00:03:12.735
or anybody else is, is the same.

00:03:12.735 --> 00:03:14.788
You know, I have the same
pressures as anyone else,

00:03:14.788 --> 00:03:16.006
there's time, there's performance,

00:03:16.006 --> 00:03:18.184
there's financial, I mean, there are,

00:03:18.184 --> 00:03:19.017
you know, there's deadlines.

00:03:19.017 --> 00:03:21.018
My pressures are not unique.

00:03:21.018 --> 00:03:23.909
The situations may be
different, or, you know,

00:03:23.909 --> 00:03:26.729
but everybody has the
same kinds of pressures.

00:03:26.729 --> 00:03:30.477
But what I found, or
what I find fascinating,

00:03:30.477 --> 00:03:33.112
is the interpretation for the stimuli,

00:03:33.112 --> 00:03:34.363
if, let me explain.

00:03:34.363 --> 00:03:36.888
So I was watching the Olympics,

00:03:36.888 --> 00:03:39.754
this last summer
Olympics, and I was amazed

00:03:39.754 --> 00:03:41.455
at how bad the questions were that

00:03:41.455 --> 00:03:44.068
the reporters would ask all the athletes.

00:03:44.068 --> 00:03:46.963
And almost always they
would ask the same question,

00:03:46.963 --> 00:03:49.660
whether they were about to compete,

00:03:49.660 --> 00:03:52.801
or after they competed:
"Were you nervous?"

00:03:52.801 --> 00:03:53.838
Right?

00:03:53.838 --> 00:03:57.171
And to a T, all the athletes went, "No."

00:03:58.336 --> 00:03:59.434
Right?

00:03:59.434 --> 00:04:02.917
And what I realized, is it's
not that they're not nervous,

00:04:02.917 --> 00:04:04.171
it's their interpretation of what's

00:04:04.171 --> 00:04:05.319
happening in their bodies, I mean,

00:04:05.319 --> 00:04:06.431
what happens when you're nervous?

00:04:06.431 --> 00:04:07.264
Right?

00:04:07.264 --> 00:04:09.104
Your heart rate starts
to go, (sighs) you're,

00:04:09.104 --> 00:04:10.247
you know, you sort of get a little tense,

00:04:10.247 --> 00:04:11.493
you get a little sweaty, right?

00:04:11.493 --> 00:04:13.485
You have expectation of what's coming,

00:04:13.485 --> 00:04:15.743
and we interpret that as "I'm nervous."

00:04:15.743 --> 00:04:17.730
Now what's the interpretation of excited?

00:04:17.730 --> 00:04:20.024
Your heart rate starts to go, you become,

00:04:20.024 --> 00:04:21.880
you're anticipating what's coming, right?

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:23.177
You get a little sort of like, tense,

00:04:23.177 --> 00:04:25.137
it's all the same thing,
it's the same stimuli.

00:04:25.137 --> 00:04:27.102
Except these athletes, these
Olympic quality athletes

00:04:27.102 --> 00:04:30.168
have learned to interpret the stimuli

00:04:30.168 --> 00:04:33.678
that the rest of us would say
is "nervous" as "excited."

00:04:33.678 --> 00:04:34.868
They all said the same thing,

00:04:34.868 --> 00:04:37.053
"No, I'm not nervous, I'm excited."

00:04:37.053 --> 00:04:40.741
And so I've actually practiced
it just to tell myself

00:04:40.741 --> 00:04:42.611
when I start to get nervous,
that this is excitement.

00:04:42.611 --> 00:04:43.966
- Yeah.
- You know?

00:04:43.966 --> 00:04:46.736
And so where when you--
I used to speak in front

00:04:46.736 --> 00:04:47.569
of a large audience,
and somebody would say,

00:04:47.569 --> 00:04:48.715
"How do you feel?"

00:04:48.715 --> 00:04:50.581
And I used to say, "A little nervous."

00:04:50.581 --> 00:04:52.265
Now when somebody says, "How do you feel?"

00:04:52.265 --> 00:04:54.329
I'm like, "Pretty excited, actually!"

00:04:54.329 --> 00:04:57.261
And it came from just
sort of telling myself,

00:04:57.261 --> 00:04:59.670
"No, no, no, this is excitement."

00:04:59.670 --> 00:05:02.576
And it becomes a little
bit automatic later on.

00:05:02.576 --> 00:05:04.826
But it's kind of a remarkable thing,

00:05:04.826 --> 00:05:07.196
to deal with pressure by
interpreting what your body

00:05:07.196 --> 00:05:10.234
is experiencing as excitement
rather than nerves.

00:05:10.234 --> 00:05:12.146
And it's really kind of effective,

00:05:12.146 --> 00:05:13.608
it makes you want to rush
forward rather than pull back,

00:05:13.608 --> 00:05:15.732
and yet it's the same experience.

00:05:15.732 --> 00:05:19.706
I talk to so many smart,
fantastic, ambitious, idealistic,

00:05:19.706 --> 00:05:23.485
hardworking kids, and
they're right out of college,

00:05:23.485 --> 00:05:25.038
they're in their entry-level jobs,

00:05:25.038 --> 00:05:27.137
and I'll ask them, "How's it goin'?"

00:05:27.137 --> 00:05:29.883
And they'll say, "I
think I'm going to quit."

00:05:29.883 --> 00:05:31.569
And I'm like, "Why?"

00:05:31.569 --> 00:05:34.851
And they say to me, "I'm
not making an impact."

00:05:34.851 --> 00:05:37.288
I'm like, "You know you've
been here eight months, right?"

00:05:37.288 --> 00:05:38.973
(audience laughing)

00:05:38.973 --> 00:05:41.677
They treat the sense of fulfillment,

00:05:41.677 --> 00:05:44.779
or even love, like it's a scavenger hunt,

00:05:44.779 --> 00:05:46.556
like it's something you look for.

00:05:46.556 --> 00:05:49.827
My millennial friends, they've
gone through so many jobs,

00:05:49.827 --> 00:05:51.993
they're either getting
fired, I mean, it was mutual.

00:05:51.993 --> 00:05:54.242
(audience laughing)

00:05:54.242 --> 00:05:58.115
Or they're quitting because
they're not making an impact,

00:05:58.115 --> 00:05:59.793
or they're not finding the
thing they're looking for,

00:05:59.793 --> 00:06:02.714
they're not feeling fulfilled,
as if it's a scavenger hunt.

00:06:02.714 --> 00:06:05.214
Love, a job you find joy from,

00:06:07.296 --> 00:06:09.627
is not something you discover!

00:06:09.627 --> 00:06:11.246
It's not like, "I found love!"

00:06:11.246 --> 00:06:12.777
Here it is.

00:06:12.777 --> 00:06:15.736
"I found a job I love",
that's not how it works.

00:06:15.736 --> 00:06:18.053
Both of those things require hard work,

00:06:18.053 --> 00:06:20.205
you are in love because you work very hard

00:06:20.205 --> 00:06:24.123
every single day of your
life to stay in love.

00:06:24.123 --> 00:06:27.328
You find a job that
brings you ultimate joy

00:06:27.328 --> 00:06:29.819
because you work hard every single day

00:06:29.819 --> 00:06:33.431
to serve those around you,
and you maintain that joy,

00:06:33.431 --> 00:06:35.603
it's not a discovery!

00:06:35.603 --> 00:06:38.062
But the problem is the
sense of impatience!

00:06:38.062 --> 00:06:40.072
It's as if an entire generation

00:06:40.072 --> 00:06:41.647
is standing at the foot of a mountain,

00:06:41.647 --> 00:06:44.146
they know exactly what they
want, they can see the summit,

00:06:44.146 --> 00:06:46.082
what they can't see is the mountain.

00:06:46.082 --> 00:06:48.499
This large, immovable object.

00:06:49.602 --> 00:06:51.527
That doesn't mean you
have to do your time,

00:06:51.527 --> 00:06:52.953
that's not what I'm talking about.

00:06:52.953 --> 00:06:55.383
Take a helicopter, climb, I don't care,

00:06:55.383 --> 00:06:57.061
but there's still a mountain.

00:06:57.061 --> 00:07:01.506
Life, career fulfillment,
relationships, are journeys.

00:07:01.506 --> 00:07:04.530
The problem is, this entire generation

00:07:04.530 --> 00:07:07.105
has an institutionalized
sense of impatience,

00:07:07.105 --> 00:07:09.523
and do they have the
patience to go on the journey

00:07:09.523 --> 00:07:13.049
to maintain love, to feel fulfilled,

00:07:13.049 --> 00:07:14.512
or do they just quit, and on to the next,

00:07:14.512 --> 00:07:16.103
dump, and on to the next?

00:07:16.103 --> 00:07:18.428
Ghost, and on to the next.

00:07:18.428 --> 00:07:22.178
In the eighteenth century
there was something

00:07:23.296 --> 00:07:26.129
that spread across Europe and eventually

00:07:26.129 --> 00:07:29.255
made it's way to America,
called Puerperal fever,

00:07:29.255 --> 00:07:33.284
also known as the 'black
death of childbed'.

00:07:33.284 --> 00:07:37.036
Basically what was happening
is women were giving birth

00:07:37.036 --> 00:07:41.203
and they would die within
48 hours after giving birth.

00:07:42.121 --> 00:07:46.476
This black death of childbirth
was the ravage of Europe

00:07:46.476 --> 00:07:49.252
and it got worse, and worse, and worse

00:07:49.252 --> 00:07:52.070
over the course of over a century.

00:07:52.070 --> 00:07:56.237
In some hospitals it was high
as 70% of women who gave birth

00:07:58.115 --> 00:08:01.372
who would die as a result of giving birth.

00:08:01.372 --> 00:08:04.403
But this was the Renaissance,
this was the time

00:08:04.403 --> 00:08:08.156
of empirical data and science,
and we had thrown away

00:08:08.156 --> 00:08:10.745
things like tradition and mysticism.

00:08:10.745 --> 00:08:13.023
These were men of science,
these were doctors.

00:08:13.023 --> 00:08:15.490
And these doctors and men of science

00:08:15.490 --> 00:08:18.106
wanted to study and
try and find the reason

00:08:18.106 --> 00:08:21.425
for this black death of childbed,

00:08:21.425 --> 00:08:23.424
and so they got to work studying.

00:08:23.424 --> 00:08:27.591
They would study the corpses
of the women who had died,

00:08:28.873 --> 00:08:31.078
and in the morning they
would conduct autopsies,

00:08:31.078 --> 00:08:33.230
and then in the afternoon they would go

00:08:33.230 --> 00:08:36.056
and deliver babies and
finish their rounds.

00:08:36.056 --> 00:08:39.106
And it wasn't until
somewhere in the mid 1800's

00:08:39.106 --> 00:08:41.868
that Doctor Oliver Wendell Holmes,

00:08:41.868 --> 00:08:45.564
father of Supreme Court
Justice Oliver Wendell Holmes,

00:08:45.564 --> 00:08:47.916
realized, that all of these doctors

00:08:47.916 --> 00:08:50.020
who were conducting
autopsies in the morning

00:08:50.020 --> 00:08:52.662
weren't washing their hands before

00:08:52.662 --> 00:08:55.815
they delivered babies in the afternoon.

00:08:55.815 --> 00:08:59.982
And he pointed it out, and said,
"Guys, you're the problem."

00:09:00.951 --> 00:09:05.118
And they ignored him, and
called him crazy, for 30 years.

00:09:07.389 --> 00:09:09.494
Until finally somebody realized

00:09:09.494 --> 00:09:13.661
that if they simply washed
their hands, it would go away.

00:09:14.881 --> 00:09:16.829
And that's exactly what happened.

00:09:16.829 --> 00:09:18.634
When they started
sterilizing their instruments

00:09:18.634 --> 00:09:19.846
and washing their hands,

00:09:19.846 --> 00:09:23.642
the black death of childbed disappeared.

00:09:23.642 --> 00:09:26.303
My point is, the lesson here is,

00:09:26.303 --> 00:09:29.326
sometimes, you're the problem.

00:09:29.326 --> 00:09:31.377
(audience laughing)

00:09:31.377 --> 00:09:33.520
We've seen this happen all too recently

00:09:33.520 --> 00:09:38.015
with our new men of science
and empirical studiers,

00:09:38.015 --> 00:09:40.098
and these men of finance,

00:09:42.207 --> 00:09:44.434
who are smarter than the rest of us

00:09:44.434 --> 00:09:46.570
until the thing collapsed.

00:09:46.570 --> 00:09:49.921
And they blamed everything
else except themselves.

00:09:49.921 --> 00:09:54.283
And my point is take
accountability for your actions.

00:09:54.283 --> 00:09:56.271
You can take all the credit in the world

00:09:56.271 --> 00:09:57.972
for the things that you do right,

00:09:57.972 --> 00:10:00.759
as long as you also take responsibility

00:10:00.759 --> 00:10:04.391
for the things you do wrong,
it must be a balanced equation.

00:10:04.391 --> 00:10:07.229
You don't get it one
way and not the other,

00:10:07.229 --> 00:10:11.367
you get to take credit when
you also take accountability.

00:10:11.367 --> 00:10:14.038
I spoke at an education
summit for Microsoft,

00:10:14.038 --> 00:10:17.478
I also spoke at an
education summit for Apple.

00:10:17.478 --> 00:10:20.632
At the education summit for Microsoft,

00:10:20.632 --> 00:10:23.141
I would say that 70% of the executives

00:10:23.141 --> 00:10:26.186
spent about 70% of their presentations

00:10:26.186 --> 00:10:28.833
talking about how to beat Apple.

00:10:28.833 --> 00:10:30.888
(audience laughing)

00:10:30.888 --> 00:10:34.573
At the Apple education
summit 100% of the executives

00:10:34.573 --> 00:10:37.865
spent 100% of their presentations

00:10:37.865 --> 00:10:40.004
talking about how to help teachers teach,

00:10:40.004 --> 00:10:41.791
and how to help students learn.

00:10:41.791 --> 00:10:45.735
One is playing this way,
and one is playing that way.

00:10:45.735 --> 00:10:49.810
One is playing finite, and
the other is playing infinite.

00:10:49.810 --> 00:10:51.803
Guess which one gets frustrated?

00:10:51.803 --> 00:10:53.990
(audience laughing)

00:10:53.990 --> 00:10:57.444
So at the end of my talk at
Microsoft they gave me a gift,

00:10:57.444 --> 00:10:59.759
they gave me the new
Zune, when it was a thing.

00:10:59.759 --> 00:11:02.759
(audience laughing)

00:11:04.230 --> 00:11:08.161
And let me tell ya, this
thing was spectacular.

00:11:08.161 --> 00:11:11.286
It was the most elegant piece
of technology I've ever used.

00:11:11.286 --> 00:11:13.585
The user interface was incredible,

00:11:13.585 --> 00:11:17.657
the design was spectacular,
I absolutely loved it.

00:11:17.657 --> 00:11:20.477
It was easy to use, and it was bright,

00:11:20.477 --> 00:11:23.261
and gorgeous, and fantastic,
it didn't work on iTunes,

00:11:23.261 --> 00:11:25.164
which is a different problem,
so I couldn't use it,

00:11:25.164 --> 00:11:27.890
but it was amazing.
(audience laughing)

00:11:27.890 --> 00:11:30.370
And elegant, my God, it was elegant.

00:11:30.370 --> 00:11:32.938
So I'm sitting in the back of the taxi

00:11:32.938 --> 00:11:34.647
with a very senior Apple executive,

00:11:34.647 --> 00:11:36.456
sort of employee number 12 kind of guy,

00:11:36.456 --> 00:11:38.758
and you know, I like to stir pots,

00:11:38.758 --> 00:11:42.574
so I turn to him, I said, "You know,

00:11:42.574 --> 00:11:45.962
"Microsoft gave me their new Zune,

00:11:45.962 --> 00:11:50.203
"and it is so much better
than your iPod touch."

00:11:50.203 --> 00:11:52.262
(audience laughing)

00:11:52.262 --> 00:11:56.197
And he turned to me and he
said, "I have no doubt."

00:11:56.197 --> 00:11:58.080
Conversation over.

00:11:58.080 --> 00:12:01.080
(audience laughing)

00:12:02.014 --> 00:12:04.076
Because the infinite player understands,

00:12:04.076 --> 00:12:06.315
sometimes you're ahead, and
sometimes you're behind.

00:12:06.315 --> 00:12:08.547
Sometimes your product is
better, and sometimes it's worse.

00:12:08.547 --> 00:12:11.278
The goal isn't to be the best every day,

00:12:11.278 --> 00:12:15.215
the goal isn't to outdo
your competition every day,

00:12:15.215 --> 00:12:17.024
that's a finite construction.

00:12:17.024 --> 00:12:19.378
If I had said to Microsoft,
"I've got the new iPod Touch

00:12:19.378 --> 00:12:20.862
"and it's so much better than your Zune",

00:12:20.862 --> 00:12:23.271
they would have said, "Can
we see it, what does it do?"

00:12:23.271 --> 00:12:25.406
React, react, react, react.

00:12:25.406 --> 00:12:29.520
Finite players play to beat
the people around them.

00:12:29.520 --> 00:12:33.687
Infinite players play to
be better than themselves.

00:12:35.063 --> 00:12:36.902
To wake up every single day and say,

00:12:36.902 --> 00:12:38.993
how can we make our
company a better version

00:12:38.993 --> 00:12:41.763
of itself today than it was yesterday?

00:12:41.763 --> 00:12:44.004
How can we create a product this week

00:12:44.004 --> 00:12:46.141
that's better than the
product we created last week?

00:12:46.141 --> 00:12:49.027
We also have to play the infinite game.

00:12:49.027 --> 00:12:50.805
It's not about being ranked number one,

00:12:50.805 --> 00:12:52.829
it's not about having
more followers on Twitter

00:12:52.829 --> 00:12:55.771
than your friends, it's
not about outdoing anyone.

00:12:55.771 --> 00:12:58.663
It's about how to outdo yourself.

00:12:58.663 --> 00:13:00.460
It's not about selling more books,

00:13:00.460 --> 00:13:02.700
or getting more TED
views than somebody else,

00:13:02.700 --> 00:13:04.805
it's about how to make sure that the work

00:13:04.805 --> 00:13:06.271
that you're producing is better

00:13:06.271 --> 00:13:09.018
than the work you produced before.

00:13:09.018 --> 00:13:11.131
You are your competition.

00:13:11.131 --> 00:13:14.379
And that is what ensures you
stay in the game the longest,

00:13:14.379 --> 00:13:17.112
and that is what ensures you find joy.

00:13:17.112 --> 00:13:20.612
Because the joy comes not from comparison,

00:13:22.161 --> 00:13:23.788
but from advancement.

00:13:23.788 --> 00:13:25.760
- When are you at your best?

00:13:25.760 --> 00:13:28.580
- I'm at my best when I'm around people

00:13:28.580 --> 00:13:29.750
who believe what I believe.

00:13:29.750 --> 00:13:32.917
I know it seems silly, but I try very,

00:13:33.949 --> 00:13:35.971
very hard to sort of stack the deck.

00:13:35.971 --> 00:13:38.033
You know, to put myself
in a position of strength.

00:13:38.033 --> 00:13:41.094
So for example, you know,
somebody asked me just yesterday,

00:13:41.094 --> 00:13:43.931
have you ever had sort of a
bad, you know, engagement?

00:13:43.931 --> 00:13:45.872
And I was thinking to myself,
and I'm like, not really.

00:13:45.872 --> 00:13:47.619
But it's not because I'm some sort of,

00:13:47.619 --> 00:13:50.273
sort of genius or anything,
anything like that,

00:13:50.273 --> 00:13:51.626
it's because I stack the deck.

00:13:51.626 --> 00:13:53.798
It's because I want to be there,

00:13:53.798 --> 00:13:55.304
I want to be around
people who want me there.

00:13:55.304 --> 00:13:57.127
In other words, if I'm
somebody's tenth choice,

00:13:57.127 --> 00:13:59.432
and like, you know, I'll
probably turn it down.

00:13:59.432 --> 00:14:02.892
Whereas if I'm their first
choice, they really want me there

00:14:02.892 --> 00:14:05.434
and so I'm more likely to
have a good engagement,

00:14:05.434 --> 00:14:07.566
they're supportive of me,
and I'm supportive of them.

00:14:07.566 --> 00:14:10.746
And so yeah, I'm at my
best when I stack the deck,

00:14:10.746 --> 00:14:12.461
when I choose to be in an environment

00:14:12.461 --> 00:14:14.642
where my strengths are there.

00:14:14.642 --> 00:14:18.709
Nelson Mandela is a particularly special

00:14:18.709 --> 00:14:21.416
case study in the leadership world,

00:14:21.416 --> 00:14:25.235
because he is universally
regarded as a great leader.

00:14:25.235 --> 00:14:27.175
You can take other personalities

00:14:27.175 --> 00:14:29.167
and depending on the nation you go to,

00:14:29.167 --> 00:14:31.161
we have different opinions
about other personalities,

00:14:31.161 --> 00:14:33.320
but Nelson Mandela, across the world,

00:14:33.320 --> 00:14:36.273
is universally regarded as a great leader.

00:14:36.273 --> 00:14:39.602
He was actually the son of a tribal chief,

00:14:39.602 --> 00:14:41.685
and he was asked one day,

00:14:42.656 --> 00:14:45.746
"How did you learn to be a great leader?"

00:14:45.746 --> 00:14:48.726
And he responded that he
would go with his father

00:14:48.726 --> 00:14:52.779
to tribal meetings, and
he remembers two things

00:14:52.779 --> 00:14:55.415
when his father would
meet with other elders.

00:14:55.415 --> 00:14:58.665
One, they would always sit in a circle.

00:15:00.234 --> 00:15:04.317
And two, his father was
always the last to speak.

00:15:06.274 --> 00:15:07.817
You will be told your whole life

00:15:07.817 --> 00:15:09.336
that you need to learn to listen,

00:15:09.336 --> 00:15:13.008
I would say that you need to
learn to be the last to speak.

00:15:13.008 --> 00:15:15.736
I see it in boardrooms
every day of the week.

00:15:15.736 --> 00:15:18.088
Even people who consider
themselves good leaders,

00:15:18.088 --> 00:15:19.680
who may actually be decent leaders,

00:15:19.680 --> 00:15:22.166
will walk into a room and
say, "Here's the problem,

00:15:22.166 --> 00:15:23.436
"here's what I think, but I'm
interested in your opinion,

00:15:23.436 --> 00:15:25.061
"let's go around the room."

00:15:25.061 --> 00:15:26.216
It's too late.

00:15:26.216 --> 00:15:29.241
The skill to hold your
opinions to yourself

00:15:29.241 --> 00:15:32.336
until everyone has spoken does two things.

00:15:32.336 --> 00:15:34.804
One, it gives everybody else the feeling

00:15:34.804 --> 00:15:38.169
that they have been heard,
it gives everyone else

00:15:38.169 --> 00:15:41.525
the ability to feel that
they have contributed.

00:15:41.525 --> 00:15:44.352
And two, you get the benefit of hearing

00:15:44.352 --> 00:15:45.905
what everybody else has to think

00:15:45.905 --> 00:15:47.374
before you render your opinion.

00:15:47.374 --> 00:15:51.017
The skill is really to keep
your opinions to yourself.

00:15:51.017 --> 00:15:54.466
If you agree with somebody, don't nod yes.

00:15:54.466 --> 00:15:57.758
If you disagree with
somebody, don't nod no.

00:15:57.758 --> 00:15:59.963
Simply sit there, take it all in,

00:15:59.963 --> 00:16:01.283
and the only thing you're allowed to do

00:16:01.283 --> 00:16:05.353
is ask questions, so that you
can understand what they mean,

00:16:05.353 --> 00:16:07.555
and why they have the
opinion that they have,

00:16:07.555 --> 00:16:11.305
you must understand from
where they are speaking.

00:16:11.305 --> 00:16:13.884
Why they have the opinion they have,

00:16:13.884 --> 00:16:16.404
not just what they are saying.

00:16:16.404 --> 00:16:20.140
And at the end, you will get your turn.

00:16:20.140 --> 00:16:22.223
It sounds easy, it's not.

00:16:23.671 --> 00:16:25.845
Practice being the last to speak,

00:16:25.845 --> 00:16:28.222
that's what Nelson Mandela did.

00:16:28.222 --> 00:16:30.165
Every decision we make in our lives

00:16:30.165 --> 00:16:32.935
as individuals or as organizations,

00:16:32.935 --> 00:16:35.115
is a piece of communication,

00:16:35.115 --> 00:16:37.492
it's our way of saying
something about who we are,

00:16:37.492 --> 00:16:39.023
and what we believe.

00:16:39.023 --> 00:16:41.107
This is why authenticity matters.

00:16:41.107 --> 00:16:43.368
This is why you have to say and do

00:16:43.368 --> 00:16:46.192
the things you actually believe.

00:16:46.192 --> 00:16:48.101
Because the things you say and do

00:16:48.101 --> 00:16:50.351
are symbols of who you are.

00:16:51.533 --> 00:16:53.698
And we look for those symbols so we can

00:16:53.698 --> 00:16:55.810
find people who believe what we believe,

00:16:55.810 --> 00:16:57.709
our very survival depends on it.

00:16:57.709 --> 00:16:59.284
So if you're putting out false symbols,

00:16:59.284 --> 00:17:01.243
you will attract people to those symbols,

00:17:01.243 --> 00:17:04.492
but you won't be able
to form trust with them.

00:17:04.492 --> 00:17:07.573
This is what Tiger Woods
did to us, he lied!

00:17:07.573 --> 00:17:09.028
He lied.

00:17:09.028 --> 00:17:12.521
He told us what he
thought we wanted to hear,

00:17:12.521 --> 00:17:14.782
and it was great, and we were drawn to it,

00:17:14.782 --> 00:17:16.955
and all of us who kind of like that idea

00:17:16.955 --> 00:17:19.628
of the, sort of the good
guy, were drawn to it.

00:17:19.628 --> 00:17:21.950
Until we found out it was a lie.

00:17:21.950 --> 00:17:24.581
He could have been the bad boy of golf,

00:17:24.581 --> 00:17:27.093
he could have had all
the same endorsements,

00:17:27.093 --> 00:17:28.956
and had a fantastic career,
and still been hailed

00:17:28.956 --> 00:17:31.583
as one of the great athletes of our day.

00:17:31.583 --> 00:17:33.850
But he didn't, he chose to lie.

00:17:33.850 --> 00:17:36.149
Good luck forming trust again, Tiger.

00:17:36.149 --> 00:17:37.483
We don't believe you.

00:17:37.483 --> 00:17:39.082
We don't trust you.

00:17:39.082 --> 00:17:41.521
The goal of putting something out there,

00:17:41.521 --> 00:17:44.742
if you say what you believe,
and you do what you believe,

00:17:44.742 --> 00:17:47.382
you will attract people who
believe what you believe.

00:17:47.382 --> 00:17:49.186
If you go to one of your friends,

00:17:49.186 --> 00:17:50.537
and you say to one of your friends,

00:17:50.537 --> 00:17:53.029
"How would you like me to dress

00:17:53.029 --> 00:17:55.617
"so that you'll like me better?"

00:17:55.617 --> 00:17:57.753
How would you want me to address you,

00:17:57.753 --> 00:18:01.249
how do you want me to speak,
so that you'll like me more?

00:18:01.249 --> 00:18:02.082
Right?

00:18:03.071 --> 00:18:04.449
Your friends are going to
look at you and be like,

00:18:04.449 --> 00:18:06.094
what the, what are you talking about?

00:18:06.094 --> 00:18:07.600
You're like, "Come on, come on, come on,

00:18:07.600 --> 00:18:10.765
"what should I wear, so that
you'll find me more appealing,

00:18:10.765 --> 00:18:12.974
"and how would you like me to speak to you

00:18:12.974 --> 00:18:14.503
"so that you'll like me more?"

00:18:14.503 --> 00:18:16.006
And your friends are going to tell you,

00:18:16.006 --> 00:18:18.970
"Just be yourself, that's why I like you,

00:18:18.970 --> 00:18:20.687
"I don't care, just be yourself."

00:18:20.687 --> 00:18:22.423
Now think about what we do in industry.

00:18:22.423 --> 00:18:23.277
What do we do?

00:18:23.277 --> 00:18:25.069
We do market research, and we go up

00:18:25.069 --> 00:18:26.964
and we ask the customers,
"What kind of things,

00:18:26.964 --> 00:18:28.917
"what style should we speak to you?

00:18:28.917 --> 00:18:30.295
"How should we decorate ourselves,

00:18:30.295 --> 00:18:31.584
"what kind of things are you drawn to,

00:18:31.584 --> 00:18:32.483
"so that we can do those things

00:18:32.483 --> 00:18:33.481
"so that you'll like us more?"

00:18:33.481 --> 00:18:35.187
It's just as ridiculous.

00:18:35.187 --> 00:18:36.920
It's just as ridiculous.

00:18:36.920 --> 00:18:38.880
Organizations should say and do

00:18:38.880 --> 00:18:40.548
the things they actually believe,

00:18:40.548 --> 00:18:43.592
and they will attract people
who believe what they believe.

00:18:43.592 --> 00:18:46.701
Or, they can choose to lie,
and at the slightest hint

00:18:46.701 --> 00:18:50.122
that they might be
lying, cynicism sets in.

00:18:50.122 --> 00:18:51.526
And people start saying,

00:18:51.526 --> 00:18:53.764
I'm not sure I can trust these guys

00:18:53.764 --> 00:18:55.592
because there's not a lot of consistency

00:18:55.592 --> 00:18:57.163
in all the things they say and do,

00:18:57.163 --> 00:18:59.432
which means they can't have
a very strong belief set,

00:18:59.432 --> 00:19:01.088
or they're lying to me.

00:19:01.088 --> 00:19:03.600
And we call them inauthentic.

00:19:03.600 --> 00:19:06.610
The entire process of asking
other people who we should be

00:19:06.610 --> 00:19:09.217
is inauthentic, that's hilarious to me!

00:19:09.217 --> 00:19:11.612
All these positioning studies
we do are inherently--

00:19:11.612 --> 00:19:13.894
We're going to do a study
to find out from people

00:19:13.894 --> 00:19:16.735
so we can be more
authentic, that's hilarious.

00:19:16.735 --> 00:19:18.410
(audience laughing)

00:19:18.410 --> 00:19:19.874
Say and do what you actually believe

00:19:19.874 --> 00:19:21.130
and the symbols you put out there,

00:19:21.130 --> 00:19:22.521
the things you say and the things you do,

00:19:22.521 --> 00:19:25.935
those red hats are ways
that people can find you.

00:19:25.935 --> 00:19:28.535
What you have the ability to do

00:19:28.535 --> 00:19:31.532
as designers, is create those symbols,

00:19:31.532 --> 00:19:34.607
and allow people to use those things

00:19:34.607 --> 00:19:36.934
to say something about who they are.

00:19:36.934 --> 00:19:38.942
Work for companies, work for clients,

00:19:38.942 --> 00:19:43.814
work for people who you
believe what they believe.

00:19:43.814 --> 00:19:47.906
Show up and feel a part of
something bigger than yourself.

00:19:47.906 --> 00:19:52.836
And your part is to put what
they believe into pictures,

00:19:52.836 --> 00:19:55.118
and words, and symbols, and graphics,

00:19:55.118 --> 00:19:57.172
so that other people can use those things

00:19:57.172 --> 00:19:59.218
to say something about who they are.

00:19:59.218 --> 00:20:01.608
People put Harley Davidson
logos on their body

00:20:01.608 --> 00:20:04.185
to say something about who
they are, corporate logo.

00:20:04.185 --> 00:20:06.872
Ain't no Procter and Gambles
tattooed on anybody's arm.

00:20:06.872 --> 00:20:08.549
(audience laughing)

00:20:08.549 --> 00:20:11.594
Because Harley means something,
they stand for something.

00:20:11.594 --> 00:20:13.021
People put that tattoo on there

00:20:13.021 --> 00:20:14.872
not to tell you that
they own a motorcycle,

00:20:14.872 --> 00:20:16.547
they put that tattoo there to tell you

00:20:16.547 --> 00:20:18.360
something about themselves.

00:20:18.360 --> 00:20:21.241
Do you ever see anybody with a Mac laptop

00:20:21.241 --> 00:20:23.737
put a sticker over that
beautiful shining apple?

00:20:23.737 --> 00:20:25.839
Ain't never going to happen!

00:20:25.839 --> 00:20:28.937
Then how will you know who I am?

00:20:28.937 --> 00:20:31.333
Did you ever see anybody
with a PC break out

00:20:31.333 --> 00:20:33.075
the Windex to clean out their computer?

00:20:33.075 --> 00:20:34.119
Mac people?

00:20:34.119 --> 00:20:35.459
(breathing heavily)

00:20:35.459 --> 00:20:37.123
(audience laughing)

00:20:37.123 --> 00:20:38.464
Have you ever seen a dirty Mac?

00:20:38.464 --> 00:20:39.887
Doesn't exist.

00:20:39.887 --> 00:20:40.955
Does not exist.

00:20:40.955 --> 00:20:41.788
Why?

00:20:41.788 --> 00:20:43.106
Because it's who I am.

00:20:43.106 --> 00:20:44.471
These are symbols we use.

00:20:44.471 --> 00:20:47.337
The companies that are crystal
clear on what they believe,

00:20:47.337 --> 00:20:49.356
and they're disciplined in how they do it,

00:20:49.356 --> 00:20:50.666
and they're consistent in what they do,

00:20:50.666 --> 00:20:52.644
and everything they say,
and everything they do

00:20:52.644 --> 00:20:55.862
serves as a symbol of the
set of values and beliefs,

00:20:55.862 --> 00:20:59.602
we use those symbols to say
something about who we are.

00:20:59.602 --> 00:21:01.422
We surround ourselves with the people,

00:21:01.422 --> 00:21:02.573
and the products, and the brands,

00:21:02.573 --> 00:21:05.407
that say something about who we are.

00:21:05.407 --> 00:21:08.064
And when we can find
the people who believe

00:21:08.064 --> 00:21:10.776
what we believe, we're
weirdly drawn to them,

00:21:10.776 --> 00:21:14.300
because our very survival depends on it.

00:21:14.300 --> 00:21:15.358
We need it.

00:21:15.358 --> 00:21:17.777
And so the more you can give of yourself,

00:21:17.777 --> 00:21:19.295
the more you can give of what you believe,

00:21:19.295 --> 00:21:21.136
the more you can
discipline, with discipline,

00:21:21.136 --> 00:21:23.534
say and do the things
you actually believe,

00:21:23.534 --> 00:21:25.228
strange things start to happen.

00:21:25.228 --> 00:21:27.252
- What are your thoughts,
and what's your approach

00:21:27.252 --> 00:21:30.238
on finding, and building upon passions?

00:21:30.238 --> 00:21:33.984
- Passion is not an actionable word.

00:21:33.984 --> 00:21:36.332
It's correct, you know,
that those who do the things

00:21:36.332 --> 00:21:38.079
that they're passionate about do better,

00:21:38.079 --> 00:21:39.834
but it's not helpful advice.

00:21:39.834 --> 00:21:42.654
And so the question is,
where does passion come from?

00:21:42.654 --> 00:21:45.857
Passion is a result, passion is an energy,

00:21:45.857 --> 00:21:48.075
passion is the feeling you have

00:21:48.075 --> 00:21:51.637
when you're engaged in
something that you love.

00:21:51.637 --> 00:21:53.545
Passion is the feeling you have

00:21:53.545 --> 00:21:57.027
that you would probably do
this for free, you know?

00:21:57.027 --> 00:21:59.235
And you can't believe
somebody pays you to do it.

00:21:59.235 --> 00:22:00.124
You know?

00:22:00.124 --> 00:22:03.266
And I think we mistake that
passion is something we do

00:22:03.266 --> 00:22:05.387
in our private lives,
but it shouldn't be done,

00:22:05.387 --> 00:22:07.372
you know, in our careers, for example.

00:22:07.372 --> 00:22:09.420
And I'm a firm believer
that you are who you are,

00:22:09.420 --> 00:22:11.318
and anybody who says,
I'm different at home

00:22:11.318 --> 00:22:13.110
than I am at work, and one of
those two places you're lying.

00:22:13.110 --> 00:22:16.016
And the goal is to make everything you do,

00:22:16.016 --> 00:22:17.398
at home and at work,

00:22:17.398 --> 00:22:19.813
something that you have excitement to do.

00:22:19.813 --> 00:22:22.114
So how do you find the things
that you're excited to do?

00:22:22.114 --> 00:22:24.519
Well it's actually easier than you think.

00:22:24.519 --> 00:22:27.033
What are the things that you love to do?

00:22:27.033 --> 00:22:29.424
What are the things that
you would do for free?

00:22:29.424 --> 00:22:30.258
You know?

00:22:30.258 --> 00:22:33.528
How can you recreate that
feeling, and paid for it?

00:22:33.528 --> 00:22:35.420
So what are the things that
I do on the weekend, right?

00:22:35.420 --> 00:22:38.682
I love, I'm very involved
in the art world.

00:22:38.682 --> 00:22:41.047
I love to go to museums and galleries,

00:22:41.047 --> 00:22:43.669
but I love to go see
dance and performances,

00:22:43.669 --> 00:22:47.433
because I want to see how others
are interpreting the world.

00:22:47.433 --> 00:22:48.838
So that inspires me.

00:22:48.838 --> 00:22:51.401
New ideas, new thoughts, new
ways of looking at the world

00:22:51.401 --> 00:22:53.753
are the things that
interest me, privately,

00:22:53.753 --> 00:22:55.262
and I seek it out and pay money for it.

00:22:55.262 --> 00:22:56.095
Right?

00:22:56.095 --> 00:22:58.827
So does that mean that I have
to have a career in the arts?

00:22:58.827 --> 00:22:59.660
No.

00:22:59.660 --> 00:23:00.902
It means I have to have a career

00:23:00.902 --> 00:23:02.690
where new ideas are explored,

00:23:02.690 --> 00:23:05.935
where people are experimenting
and trying things out,

00:23:05.935 --> 00:23:08.443
and I have to explore new
ideas and try things out,

00:23:08.443 --> 00:23:10.331
and I'm just as excited
to go to work every day

00:23:10.331 --> 00:23:13.473
as I am to go do something
on a Saturday night.

00:23:13.473 --> 00:23:16.325
And so the idea of finding your passion

00:23:16.325 --> 00:23:19.403
is ironically simple because
you should be doing stuff

00:23:19.403 --> 00:23:23.352
that you enjoy sometimes, what
is the stuff that you enjoy,

00:23:23.352 --> 00:23:25.718
and then what is the stuff that you love?

00:23:25.718 --> 00:23:26.730
Who are the people that you love,

00:23:26.730 --> 00:23:28.517
and what do they all have in common?

00:23:28.517 --> 00:23:31.961
And how do you explain when
things don't go as we assume?

00:23:31.961 --> 00:23:35.442
Or better, how do you explain when others

00:23:35.442 --> 00:23:37.299
are able to achieve things that seem

00:23:37.299 --> 00:23:38.947
to defy all of the assumptions?

00:23:38.947 --> 00:23:42.604
For example, why is Apple so innovative?

00:23:42.604 --> 00:23:45.581
Year, after year, after year, after year,

00:23:45.581 --> 00:23:48.117
they're more innovative
than all their competition.

00:23:48.117 --> 00:23:50.496
And yet they're just a computer company,

00:23:50.496 --> 00:23:52.285
they're just like everyone else.

00:23:52.285 --> 00:23:54.525
They have the same access
to the same talent,

00:23:54.525 --> 00:23:57.288
the same agencies, the same
consultants, the same media.

00:23:57.288 --> 00:24:01.433
Then why is it that they seem
to have something different?

00:24:01.433 --> 00:24:03.824
Why is it that Martin Luther King

00:24:03.824 --> 00:24:06.055
led the civil rights movement?

00:24:06.055 --> 00:24:08.560
He wasn't the only man who suffered

00:24:08.560 --> 00:24:10.100
in a pre-civil rights America,

00:24:10.100 --> 00:24:12.691
and he certainly wasn't the
only great orator of the day,

00:24:12.691 --> 00:24:13.951
why him?

00:24:13.951 --> 00:24:16.895
And why is it that the Wright brothers

00:24:16.895 --> 00:24:20.064
were able to figure out
control powered man flight

00:24:20.064 --> 00:24:21.870
when there were certainly other teams

00:24:21.870 --> 00:24:24.737
who were better qualified, better funded,

00:24:24.737 --> 00:24:28.091
and they didn't achieve
powered man flight,

00:24:28.091 --> 00:24:30.312
and the Wright brothers beat them to it?

00:24:30.312 --> 00:24:32.346
There's something else at play here.

00:24:32.346 --> 00:24:37.290
About three and a half years
ago I made a discovery.

00:24:37.290 --> 00:24:40.957
And this discovery
profoundly changed my view

00:24:40.957 --> 00:24:43.368
on how I though the world worked,

00:24:43.368 --> 00:24:44.867
and it even profoundly changed the way

00:24:44.867 --> 00:24:46.950
in which I operate in it.

00:24:49.043 --> 00:24:51.934
As it turns out, there's a pattern.

00:24:51.934 --> 00:24:54.911
As it turns out, all the
great and inspiring leaders

00:24:54.911 --> 00:24:56.527
and organizations in the world,

00:24:56.527 --> 00:24:57.821
whether it's Apple, or Martin Luther King,

00:24:57.821 --> 00:24:59.583
or the Wright Brothers, they all think,

00:24:59.583 --> 00:25:03.115
act, and communicate the exact same way,

00:25:03.115 --> 00:25:06.556
and it's the complete
opposite to everyone else.

00:25:06.556 --> 00:25:08.556
All I did was codify it.

00:25:09.459 --> 00:25:12.639
And it's probably the
world's simplest idea.

00:25:12.639 --> 00:25:14.972
I call it the golden circle.

00:25:23.263 --> 00:25:24.513
Why, how, what.

00:25:25.998 --> 00:25:29.396
This little idea explains
why some organizations,

00:25:29.396 --> 00:25:32.686
and some leaders, are able to
inspire where others aren't.

00:25:32.686 --> 00:25:34.548
Let me define the terms really quickly.

00:25:34.548 --> 00:25:36.918
Every single person,
every single organization

00:25:36.918 --> 00:25:40.061
on the planet knows what they do, 100%.

00:25:40.061 --> 00:25:43.685
Some know how they do
it, whether you call it

00:25:43.685 --> 00:25:45.430
your differentiating value proposition,

00:25:45.430 --> 00:25:47.802
or proprietary process, or your USP,

00:25:47.802 --> 00:25:50.401
but very, very few people or organizations

00:25:50.401 --> 00:25:52.912
know why they do what they do.

00:25:52.912 --> 00:25:54.633
And by why I don't mean to make a profit.

00:25:54.633 --> 00:25:57.151
That's a result, it's always a result.

00:25:57.151 --> 00:25:58.893
By why, I mean what's your purpose,

00:25:58.893 --> 00:26:01.294
what's your cause, what's your belief.

00:26:01.294 --> 00:26:04.641
Why does your organization exist?

00:26:04.641 --> 00:26:07.274
Why do you get out of bed in the morning?

00:26:07.274 --> 00:26:09.512
And why should anyone care?

00:26:09.512 --> 00:26:11.467
Well as a result, the way that we think,

00:26:11.467 --> 00:26:13.176
the way we act, the way we communicate,

00:26:13.176 --> 00:26:14.888
is from the outside it, it's obvious.

00:26:14.888 --> 00:26:17.610
We go from the clearest
thing to the fuzziest thing.

00:26:17.610 --> 00:26:21.240
But the inspired leaders and
the inspired organizations,

00:26:21.240 --> 00:26:24.550
regardless of their size,
regardless of their industry,

00:26:24.550 --> 00:26:29.242
all think, act, and communicate
from the inside out.

00:26:29.242 --> 00:26:31.219
Let me give you an example.

00:26:31.219 --> 00:26:32.594
I use Apple because
they're easy to understand

00:26:32.594 --> 00:26:34.188
and everybody gets it.

00:26:34.188 --> 00:26:36.714
If Apple were like everyone else,

00:26:36.714 --> 00:26:40.426
a marketing message from
them might sound like this:

00:26:40.426 --> 00:26:44.452
We make great computers,
they're beautifully designed,

00:26:44.452 --> 00:26:46.658
simple to use, and user friendly.

00:26:46.658 --> 00:26:48.631
Want to buy one?

00:26:48.631 --> 00:26:49.464
Meh.

00:26:50.391 --> 00:26:52.569
And that's how most of communicate,

00:26:52.569 --> 00:26:53.642
that's how most marketing is done,

00:26:53.642 --> 00:26:54.633
that's how most sales is done,

00:26:54.633 --> 00:26:56.585
and that's how most of us
communicate interpersonally.

00:26:56.585 --> 00:26:58.896
We say what we do, we
say how we're different,

00:26:58.896 --> 00:27:01.128
or how we're better, and we
expect some sort of behavior,

00:27:01.128 --> 00:27:03.053
or purchase, or vote, something like that.

00:27:03.053 --> 00:27:05.814
Here's our new law firm,
we have the best lawyers

00:27:05.814 --> 00:27:07.368
with the biggest clients,
we have, you know,

00:27:07.368 --> 00:27:09.508
we always perform for our
clients, do business with us.

00:27:09.508 --> 00:27:11.643
Here's our new car, it
gets great gas mileage,

00:27:11.643 --> 00:27:14.958
it has, you know, leather
seats, buy our car.

00:27:14.958 --> 00:27:16.217
But it's uninspiring.

00:27:16.217 --> 00:27:19.079
Here's how Apple actually communicates:

00:27:19.079 --> 00:27:23.246
Everything we do, we believe
in challenging the status quo,

00:27:25.386 --> 00:27:27.889
we believe in thinking differently.

00:27:27.889 --> 00:27:30.383
The way we challenge the status quo

00:27:30.383 --> 00:27:32.718
is buy making our products
beautifully designed,

00:27:32.718 --> 00:27:35.040
simple to use, and user friendly.

00:27:35.040 --> 00:27:37.406
We just happen to make great computers.

00:27:37.406 --> 00:27:39.296
Want to buy one?

00:27:39.296 --> 00:27:40.965
Totally different, right?

00:27:40.965 --> 00:27:42.540
You're ready to buy a computer from me.

00:27:42.540 --> 00:27:45.048
All I did was reverse the
order of the information.

00:27:45.048 --> 00:27:47.824
What it proves to us is that
people don't buy what you do,

00:27:47.824 --> 00:27:49.479
people buy why you do it.

00:27:49.479 --> 00:27:51.657
People don't buy what you
do, they buy why you do it.

00:27:51.657 --> 00:27:55.165
This explains why every
single person in this room

00:27:55.165 --> 00:27:58.795
is perfectly comfortable
buying a computer from Apple.

00:27:58.795 --> 00:28:00.672
But we're also perfectly comfortable

00:28:00.672 --> 00:28:02.813
buying an MP3 player from Apple,

00:28:02.813 --> 00:28:05.910
or a phone from Apple,
or a DVR from Apple.

00:28:05.910 --> 00:28:08.678
But as I said before, Apple's
just a computer company,

00:28:08.678 --> 00:28:10.782
there's nothing that
distinguishes them structurally

00:28:10.782 --> 00:28:12.075
from any of their competitors.

00:28:12.075 --> 00:28:14.058
Their competitors are
all equally qualified

00:28:14.058 --> 00:28:15.567
to make all of these products.

00:28:15.567 --> 00:28:17.686
In fact, they tried a few years ago,

00:28:17.686 --> 00:28:19.711
Gateway came out with flat screen TV's.

00:28:19.711 --> 00:28:22.423
They're imminently qualified
to make flat screen TV's,

00:28:22.423 --> 00:28:24.890
they've been making flat
screen monitors for years.

00:28:24.890 --> 00:28:26.390
Nobody bought one.

00:28:29.897 --> 00:28:34.555
And Dell, Dell came out
with MP3 players and PDA's,

00:28:34.555 --> 00:28:37.536
and they make great quality products,

00:28:37.536 --> 00:28:39.659
and they can make perfectly
well-designed products,

00:28:39.659 --> 00:28:41.777
and nobody bought one.

00:28:41.777 --> 00:28:43.814
In fact talking about it
now, we can't even imagine

00:28:43.814 --> 00:28:45.769
buying an MP3 player from Dell,

00:28:45.769 --> 00:28:47.923
why would you buy an MP3
player from a computer company?

00:28:47.923 --> 00:28:49.746
But we do it every day.

00:28:49.746 --> 00:28:52.485
People don't what you do,
they buy why you do it.

00:28:52.485 --> 00:28:55.356
The goal is not to do
business with everybody

00:28:55.356 --> 00:28:59.175
who needs what you have,
the goal is to do business

00:28:59.175 --> 00:29:02.989
with people who believe what you believe.

00:29:02.989 --> 00:29:04.386
Here's the best part,

00:29:04.386 --> 00:29:07.377
none of what I'm telling
you is my opinion.

00:29:07.377 --> 00:29:09.897
It's all grounded in
the tenants of biology.

00:29:09.897 --> 00:29:12.480
Not psychology, biology.

00:29:12.480 --> 00:29:14.285
If you look at a cross
section of the human brain

00:29:14.285 --> 00:29:15.699
looking from the top down,

00:29:15.699 --> 00:29:16.856
what you see is that the human brain

00:29:16.856 --> 00:29:18.879
is actually broken into
three major components

00:29:18.879 --> 00:29:21.985
that correlate perfectly
with the golden circle.

00:29:21.985 --> 00:29:24.718
Our newest brain, our homo sapien brain,

00:29:24.718 --> 00:29:28.458
our neocortex, corresponds
with the what level.

00:29:28.458 --> 00:29:31.346
The neocortex is responsible
for all of our rational,

00:29:31.346 --> 00:29:34.282
and analytical thought, and language.

00:29:34.282 --> 00:29:37.875
The middle two sections
make up our limbic brains,

00:29:37.875 --> 00:29:39.546
and our limbic brains are responsible

00:29:39.546 --> 00:29:44.076
for all of our feelings,
like trust, and loyalty.

00:29:44.076 --> 00:29:46.327
It's also responsible
for all human behavior,

00:29:46.327 --> 00:29:51.051
all decision making, and it
has no capacity for language.

00:29:51.051 --> 00:29:53.827
In other words, when we
communicate from the outside in,

00:29:53.827 --> 00:29:55.983
yes, people can understand vast amounts

00:29:55.983 --> 00:29:57.965
of complicated information like features,

00:29:57.965 --> 00:29:59.815
and benefits, and facts, and figures,

00:29:59.815 --> 00:30:01.734
it just doesn't drive behavior.

00:30:01.734 --> 00:30:03.520
When we communicate from the inside out,

00:30:03.520 --> 00:30:06.072
we're talking directly
to the part of the brain

00:30:06.072 --> 00:30:09.010
that controls behavior,
and then we allow people

00:30:09.010 --> 00:30:12.140
to rationalize it with the
tangible things we say and do.

00:30:12.140 --> 00:30:14.298
This is where gut decisions come from.

00:30:14.298 --> 00:30:16.910
You know, sometimes you can give somebody

00:30:16.910 --> 00:30:18.501
all the facts and the figures,

00:30:18.501 --> 00:30:20.594
and they say, I know what all
the facts and details say,

00:30:20.594 --> 00:30:22.704
but it just doesn't feel right.

00:30:22.704 --> 00:30:24.944
Why would we use that verb,
it doesn't feel right?

00:30:24.944 --> 00:30:26.996
Because the part of the brain
that controls decision making

00:30:26.996 --> 00:30:30.234
doesn't control language, and
the best we can muster up is,

00:30:30.234 --> 00:30:32.688
I don't know, it just doesn't feel right.

00:30:32.688 --> 00:30:34.405
Or sometimes you say you're
leading with your heart,

00:30:34.405 --> 00:30:35.534
or you're leading with your soul.

00:30:35.534 --> 00:30:36.767
Well I hate to break it to you,

00:30:36.767 --> 00:30:39.308
those aren't other body parts
controlling your behavior,

00:30:39.308 --> 00:30:41.565
it's all happening here
in your limbic brain,

00:30:41.565 --> 00:30:42.782
the part of the brain that controls

00:30:42.782 --> 00:30:45.017
decision making and not language.

00:30:45.017 --> 00:30:47.748
But if you don't know
why you do what you do,

00:30:47.748 --> 00:30:51.117
and people respond to
why you do what you do,

00:30:51.117 --> 00:30:53.604
then how will anybody, how
will you ever get people

00:30:53.604 --> 00:30:55.893
to get vote for you, or
buy something from you,

00:30:55.893 --> 00:30:57.745
or more importantly, be loyal,

00:30:57.745 --> 00:31:01.221
and want to be a part of
what it is that you do?

00:31:01.221 --> 00:31:03.411
Again, the goal is not just to sell people

00:31:03.411 --> 00:31:05.453
who need what you have,
the goal is to sell

00:31:05.453 --> 00:31:07.431
to people who believe what you believe.

00:31:07.431 --> 00:31:10.728
The goal is not just to
hire people who need a job,

00:31:10.728 --> 00:31:13.894
it's to hire people who
believe what you believe.

00:31:13.894 --> 00:31:16.101
I always say that you know, there's,

00:31:16.101 --> 00:31:20.184
if you hire people just
because they can do a job

00:31:21.124 --> 00:31:23.061
they'll work for your money,
but if you hire people

00:31:23.061 --> 00:31:24.372
who believe what you
believe, they'll work for you

00:31:24.372 --> 00:31:26.374
with blood, and sweat, and tears.

00:31:26.374 --> 00:31:28.801
And nowhere else is there
a better example of this

00:31:28.801 --> 00:31:30.506
than with the Wright brothers.

00:31:30.506 --> 00:31:33.356
Most people don't know about
Samuel Pierpont Langley.

00:31:33.356 --> 00:31:35.799
And back in the early 20th Century,

00:31:35.799 --> 00:31:37.900
the pursuit of powered man flight

00:31:37.900 --> 00:31:40.532
was like the dot com of the
day, everybody was trying it.

00:31:40.532 --> 00:31:43.426
And Samuel Pierpont
Langley had what we assume

00:31:43.426 --> 00:31:47.198
to be the recipe for success, I mean,

00:31:47.198 --> 00:31:49.943
even now when you ask
people, why did your product,

00:31:49.943 --> 00:31:51.869
or why did your company fail,
and people always give you

00:31:51.869 --> 00:31:54.386
the same permutation of
the same three things.

00:31:54.386 --> 00:31:58.246
Unde capitalized, the wrong
people, bad market conditions,

00:31:58.246 --> 00:32:00.074
it's always the same three things.

00:32:00.074 --> 00:32:01.536
So let's explore that.

00:32:01.536 --> 00:32:05.218
Samuel Pierpont Langley
was given $50,000 dollars

00:32:05.218 --> 00:32:08.291
by the War Department to figure
out this "flying machine."

00:32:08.291 --> 00:32:09.727
Money was no problem.

00:32:09.727 --> 00:32:13.004
He held a seat at Harvard and
worked at the Smithsonian,

00:32:13.004 --> 00:32:14.991
and was extremely well connected.

00:32:14.991 --> 00:32:16.686
He knew all the big minds of the day.

00:32:16.686 --> 00:32:20.346
He hired the best minds money could find,

00:32:20.346 --> 00:32:23.043
and the market conditions were fantastic.

00:32:23.043 --> 00:32:25.559
The New York Times followed
him around everywhere,

00:32:25.559 --> 00:32:27.779
and everyone was rooting for Langley.

00:32:27.779 --> 00:32:31.060
Then how come we've never heard
of Samuel Pierpont Langley?

00:32:31.060 --> 00:32:34.687
A few hundred miles away in Dayton, Ohio,

00:32:34.687 --> 00:32:37.027
Orville and Wilber Wright, they had none

00:32:37.027 --> 00:32:40.387
of what we consider
the recipe for success.

00:32:40.387 --> 00:32:42.558
They had no money, they
paid for their dream

00:32:42.558 --> 00:32:44.733
with the proceeds from their bicycle shop.

00:32:44.733 --> 00:32:47.122
Not a single person on
the Wright brother's team

00:32:47.122 --> 00:32:50.726
had a college education,
not even Orville or Wilbur.

00:32:50.726 --> 00:32:54.029
And the New York Times
followed them around nowhere.

00:32:54.029 --> 00:32:57.602
The difference was, Orville
and Wilbur were driven

00:32:57.602 --> 00:32:59.629
by a cause, by a purpose, by a belief.

00:32:59.629 --> 00:33:01.839
They believed that if
they could figure out

00:33:01.839 --> 00:33:06.376
this flying machine, it'll
changed the course of the world.

00:33:06.376 --> 00:33:09.375
Samuel Pierpont Langley was different.

00:33:09.375 --> 00:33:11.944
He wanted to rich, and
he wanted to be famous.

00:33:11.944 --> 00:33:14.087
He was in pursuit of the result.

00:33:14.087 --> 00:33:15.618
He was in pursuit of the riches.

00:33:15.618 --> 00:33:17.917
And low and behold look what happened.

00:33:17.917 --> 00:33:21.575
The people who believed in
the Wright brothers dream

00:33:21.575 --> 00:33:23.943
worked with them with
blood, and sweat, and tears.

00:33:23.943 --> 00:33:26.017
The others just worked for the paycheck.

00:33:26.017 --> 00:33:28.076
And they tell stories of how every time

00:33:28.076 --> 00:33:30.198
the Wright Brothers went
out they would have to take

00:33:30.198 --> 00:33:31.788
five sets of parts because
that's how many times

00:33:31.788 --> 00:33:35.055
they would crash before
they came in for supper.

00:33:35.055 --> 00:33:38.222
And eventually, on December 17th, 1903

00:33:39.204 --> 00:33:41.161
the Wright brothers took flight.

00:33:41.161 --> 00:33:44.523
And no one was there
to even experience it.

00:33:44.523 --> 00:33:47.610
We found out about it a few days later.

00:33:47.610 --> 00:33:49.990
And further proof that
Langley was motivated

00:33:49.990 --> 00:33:53.587
by the wrong thing, the day the
Wright brothers took flight,

00:33:53.587 --> 00:33:54.420
he quit.

00:33:55.573 --> 00:33:58.593
He could have said, that's
an amazing discovery, guys,

00:33:58.593 --> 00:34:00.689
and I will improve upon your technology.

00:34:00.689 --> 00:34:01.989
But he didn't.

00:34:01.989 --> 00:34:03.732
He wasn't first, he didn't get rich,

00:34:03.732 --> 00:34:06.087
he didn't get famous, so he quit.

00:34:06.087 --> 00:34:09.033
People don't buy what you
do, they buy why you do it.

00:34:09.033 --> 00:34:10.779
And if you talk about what you believe

00:34:10.779 --> 00:34:13.613
you will attract those who
believe what you believe.

00:34:13.613 --> 00:34:15.344
Well why is it important to attract

00:34:15.344 --> 00:34:18.261
those who believe what you believe?

00:34:19.400 --> 00:34:21.792
Something called the law
of diffusion of innovation.

00:34:21.792 --> 00:34:22.625
And if you don't know the law,

00:34:22.625 --> 00:34:24.272
you definitely know the terminology.

00:34:24.272 --> 00:34:26.404
The first two and a half percent of

00:34:26.404 --> 00:34:28.966
our population are our innovators.

00:34:28.966 --> 00:34:31.231
The next 13 and a half percent of

00:34:31.231 --> 00:34:33.434
our population are our early adopters,

00:34:33.434 --> 00:34:36.382
the next 34 percent are
your early majority,

00:34:36.382 --> 00:34:38.769
your late majority, and your laggards.

00:34:38.769 --> 00:34:41.335
The only reason these
people by touch tone phones

00:34:41.335 --> 00:34:43.877
is because you can't buy
rotatory phones anymore.

00:34:43.877 --> 00:34:45.841
(audience laughing)

00:34:45.841 --> 00:34:49.637
We all sit at various places
at various times on the scale,

00:34:49.637 --> 00:34:51.636
but what the law of diffusion
of innovation tell us

00:34:51.636 --> 00:34:54.796
is that if you want mass market success,

00:34:54.796 --> 00:34:57.096
or mass market acceptance of an idea,

00:34:57.096 --> 00:35:01.024
you cannot have it until you
achieve this tipping point

00:35:01.024 --> 00:35:04.297
between 15 and 18 percent
market penetration.

00:35:04.297 --> 00:35:07.174
And then the system tips.

00:35:07.174 --> 00:35:08.459
And I love asking businesses,

00:35:08.459 --> 00:35:10.006
what's your conversion on new business?

00:35:10.006 --> 00:35:10.839
And they love tell you,

00:35:10.839 --> 00:35:12.656
"Oh, it's about 10 percent", proudly.

00:35:12.656 --> 00:35:14.475
Well you can trip over 10
percent of the customers.

00:35:14.475 --> 00:35:16.440
We all have about 10
percent who just "get it",

00:35:16.440 --> 00:35:17.912
that's how we describe them, right?

00:35:17.912 --> 00:35:19.722
That's like that gut feeling,
oh, they just "get it."

00:35:19.722 --> 00:35:21.432
The problem is, how do you find the ones

00:35:21.432 --> 00:35:23.335
that just get it before you're
doing business with them,

00:35:23.335 --> 00:35:25.157
versus the ones who don't get it.

00:35:25.157 --> 00:35:30.047
So it's this here, this little
gap, that you have to close,

00:35:30.047 --> 00:35:32.694
as Jefferey Moore calls
it, crossing the chasm.

00:35:32.694 --> 00:35:36.252
Because you see, the early
majority will not try something

00:35:36.252 --> 00:35:40.022
until someone else has tried it first.

00:35:40.022 --> 00:35:43.039
And these guys, the innovators,
and the early adopters,

00:35:43.039 --> 00:35:45.345
they're comfortable making
those gut decisions.

00:35:45.345 --> 00:35:48.040
They're more comfortable making
those intuitive decisions

00:35:48.040 --> 00:35:52.302
that are driven by what they
believe about the world,

00:35:52.302 --> 00:35:54.532
and not just what product is available.

00:35:54.532 --> 00:35:56.595
These are the people who stood in line

00:35:56.595 --> 00:35:58.616
for six hours to buy an iPhone
when they first came out,

00:35:58.616 --> 00:36:00.056
when you could have just
walked into the store

00:36:00.056 --> 00:36:02.173
the next week and bought
one off the shelf.

00:36:02.173 --> 00:36:04.641
These are the people who
spent $40,000 dollars

00:36:04.641 --> 00:36:07.147
on flat screen TV's when
they first came out,

00:36:07.147 --> 00:36:09.682
even though the technology
was substandard.

00:36:09.682 --> 00:36:11.775
And by the way, they didn't do it because

00:36:11.775 --> 00:36:15.611
the technology was so great,
they did it for themselves.

00:36:15.611 --> 00:36:17.889
It's because they wanted to be first.

00:36:17.889 --> 00:36:20.433
People don't buy what you
do, they buy why you do it,

00:36:20.433 --> 00:36:23.802
and what you do simply
proves what you believe.

00:36:23.802 --> 00:36:25.849
In fact, people will do the things

00:36:25.849 --> 00:36:27.656
that prove what they believe.

00:36:27.656 --> 00:36:30.252
The reason that person bought the iPhone

00:36:30.252 --> 00:36:33.996
in the first six hours, and
stood in line for six hours,

00:36:33.996 --> 00:36:37.192
was because of what they
believed about the world,

00:36:37.192 --> 00:36:39.305
and how they wanted everybody to see them.

00:36:39.305 --> 00:36:40.709
They were first.

00:36:40.709 --> 00:36:43.181
People don't buy what you
do, they buy why you do it.

00:36:43.181 --> 00:36:45.769
So let me give you a famous example,

00:36:45.769 --> 00:36:48.122
a famous failure, and a famous success

00:36:48.122 --> 00:36:50.021
of the law of diffusion of innovation.

00:36:50.021 --> 00:36:51.614
First the famous failure.

00:36:51.614 --> 00:36:53.271
It's a commercial example.

00:36:53.271 --> 00:36:55.022
As we said before a second ago,

00:36:55.022 --> 00:36:57.089
the recipe for success is
money, and the right people,

00:36:57.089 --> 00:36:58.909
and the right marketing conditions, right?

00:36:58.909 --> 00:37:00.308
You should have success then.

00:37:00.308 --> 00:37:01.769
Look at TiVo.

00:37:01.769 --> 00:37:04.733
From the time TiVo came out
about eight or nine years ago,

00:37:04.733 --> 00:37:06.930
to this current day, they are the single

00:37:06.930 --> 00:37:09.646
highest quality product on the market.

00:37:09.646 --> 00:37:11.810
Hands down, there is no dispute.

00:37:11.810 --> 00:37:14.291
They were extremely well funded,

00:37:14.291 --> 00:37:16.039
market conditions were fantastic,

00:37:16.039 --> 00:37:17.782
I mean we used TiVo as a verb.

00:37:17.782 --> 00:37:19.368
I TiVo'd stuff on my piece of junk

00:37:19.368 --> 00:37:21.728
Time Warner DVR all the time!

00:37:21.728 --> 00:37:23.291
(audience laughing)

00:37:23.291 --> 00:37:25.686
But TiVo is a commercial failure.

00:37:25.686 --> 00:37:27.701
They've never made money.

00:37:27.701 --> 00:37:30.726
And when they went IPO, their stock was at

00:37:30.726 --> 00:37:32.862
about $30 or $40 dollars,
and then plummeted,

00:37:32.862 --> 00:37:34.723
and it's never traded above $10.

00:37:34.723 --> 00:37:37.080
In fact, I don't think
it's even traded above six,

00:37:37.080 --> 00:37:39.068
except for a couple of little spikes.

00:37:39.068 --> 00:37:41.136
Because you see, when TiVo
launched their product,

00:37:41.136 --> 00:37:43.477
they told us all what they had.

00:37:43.477 --> 00:37:47.186
They said, "We have a
product that pauses live TV,

00:37:47.186 --> 00:37:50.728
"skips commercials, rewinds live TV,

00:37:50.728 --> 00:37:54.459
"and memorizes your viewing
habits without you even asking."

00:37:54.459 --> 00:37:58.333
And the cynical majority
said, "We don't believe you,

00:37:58.333 --> 00:38:02.652
"we don't need it, we don't
like it, you're scaring us."

00:38:02.652 --> 00:38:06.535
What if they had said, "If
you're the kind of person

00:38:06.535 --> 00:38:09.818
"who likes to have total control

00:38:09.818 --> 00:38:12.837
"over every aspect of your life,

00:38:12.837 --> 00:38:15.972
"boy, do we have a product for you."

00:38:15.972 --> 00:38:17.701
That pauses live TV, skips commercials,

00:38:17.701 --> 00:38:20.900
memorizes your viewing
habits, et cetera, et cetera.

00:38:20.900 --> 00:38:23.193
People don't buy what you
do, they buy why you do it,

00:38:23.193 --> 00:38:24.638
and what you do simple serves as

00:38:24.638 --> 00:38:26.745
the proof of what you believe.

00:38:26.745 --> 00:38:29.822
Now let me give you a successful example

00:38:29.822 --> 00:38:32.909
of the law of diffusion of innovation.

00:38:32.909 --> 00:38:36.826
In the summer of 1963,
250,000 people showed up

00:38:38.631 --> 00:38:43.177
on the Mall in Washington
to hear Dr. King speak.

00:38:43.177 --> 00:38:45.120
They sent out no invitations,

00:38:45.120 --> 00:38:48.832
and there was no website
to check the date.

00:38:48.832 --> 00:38:50.258
How do you do that?

00:38:50.258 --> 00:38:53.753
Well, Dr. King wasn't
the only man in America

00:38:53.753 --> 00:38:57.194
who was a great orator, he
wasn't the only man in America

00:38:57.194 --> 00:38:59.163
who suffered in a
pre-civil rights America,

00:38:59.163 --> 00:39:01.396
in fact, some of his ideas were bad!

00:39:01.396 --> 00:39:03.197
But he had a gift.

00:39:03.197 --> 00:39:05.485
He didn't go around telling people

00:39:05.485 --> 00:39:07.426
what needed to change in America,

00:39:07.426 --> 00:39:09.544
he went around and told
people what he believed.

00:39:09.544 --> 00:39:13.193
I believe, I believe, I
believe, he told people.

00:39:13.193 --> 00:39:16.715
And people who believed what
he believed took his cause

00:39:16.715 --> 00:39:18.953
and they made it their
own, and they told people.

00:39:18.953 --> 00:39:21.620
And some of those people
created structures

00:39:21.620 --> 00:39:23.734
to get the word out to even more people,

00:39:23.734 --> 00:39:27.698
and lo and behold,
250,000 people showed up

00:39:27.698 --> 00:39:31.558
on the right day, on the
right time, to hear him speak.

00:39:31.558 --> 00:39:34.475
How many of them showed up for him?

00:39:35.980 --> 00:39:36.813
Zero.

00:39:38.359 --> 00:39:40.324
They showed up for themselves.

00:39:40.324 --> 00:39:42.739
It's what they believed about America

00:39:42.739 --> 00:39:45.114
that got them to travel
on a bus for eight hours

00:39:45.114 --> 00:39:48.190
to stand in the sun in Washington
in the middle of August.

00:39:48.190 --> 00:39:50.033
It's what they believed,
and it wasn't about

00:39:50.033 --> 00:39:53.769
black versus white, 25 percent
of the audience was white!

00:39:53.769 --> 00:39:56.743
Dr. King believed that
there were two types of laws

00:39:56.743 --> 00:39:58.545
in this world, those that are made

00:39:58.545 --> 00:40:01.923
by a higher authority, and
those that are made by man.

00:40:01.923 --> 00:40:04.053
And not until all the
laws that are made by man

00:40:04.053 --> 00:40:06.175
are consistent with the laws that are made

00:40:06.175 --> 00:40:09.410
by the higher authority will
we live in a just world.

00:40:09.410 --> 00:40:11.703
It just so happens that
the civil rights movement

00:40:11.703 --> 00:40:15.698
was the perfect thing to help
him bring his cause to life.

00:40:15.698 --> 00:40:19.152
We followed him not for
him, but for ourselves.

00:40:19.152 --> 00:40:21.608
And by the way, he gave
the I Have A Dream speech,

00:40:21.608 --> 00:40:23.666
not the I Have A Plan Speech.

00:40:23.666 --> 00:40:26.589
(audience laughing)

00:40:26.589 --> 00:40:28.910
I listen to politicians now with their

00:40:28.910 --> 00:40:32.126
comprehensive 12 point plans,
they're not inspiring anybody.

00:40:32.126 --> 00:40:35.371
Because there are leaders
and there are those who lead.

00:40:35.371 --> 00:40:39.120
Leaders hold a position
of power, or authority,

00:40:39.120 --> 00:40:41.703
but those who lead, inspire us.

00:40:43.411 --> 00:40:45.685
Whether they're individuals
or organizations,

00:40:45.685 --> 00:40:49.209
we follow those who lead,
not because we have to,

00:40:49.209 --> 00:40:51.353
but because we want to.

00:40:51.353 --> 00:40:55.520
We follow those who lead not
for them, but for ourselves.

00:40:56.759 --> 00:41:00.275
And it's those who start with why

00:41:00.275 --> 00:41:04.341
that have the ability to
inspire those around them,

00:41:04.341 --> 00:41:07.597
or find others who inspire them.

00:41:07.597 --> 00:41:09.301
Thank you very much.

00:41:09.301 --> 00:41:11.551
(applause)

00:41:12.949 --> 00:41:14.740
- Thank you guys so much for watching,

00:41:14.740 --> 00:41:17.834
I made this video because Lex
van den Herik asked me to.

00:41:17.834 --> 00:41:19.452
So if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:41:19.452 --> 00:41:21.202
that you would like me to profile next,

00:41:21.202 --> 00:41:22.922
please leave it down in the comments below

00:41:22.922 --> 00:41:24.204
and I'll see what I can do.

00:41:24.204 --> 00:41:25.569
I'd also love to know which clip resonated

00:41:25.569 --> 00:41:27.606
the most with you today,
what did Simon say

00:41:27.606 --> 00:41:30.089
that had the biggest impact,
what change are you going to make

00:41:30.089 --> 00:41:32.429
to your life or business
after watching this video?

00:41:32.429 --> 00:41:34.332
Please leave it down
in the comments below,

00:41:34.332 --> 00:41:36.521
and I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:41:36.521 --> 00:41:39.007
Thank you so much for
watching, I believe in you,

00:41:39.007 --> 00:41:41.283
I hope you continue to
believe in yourself,

00:41:41.283 --> 00:41:43.616
and whatever your one word is.

00:41:43.616 --> 00:41:45.500
Much love, I'll see ya soon.

00:41:45.500 --> 00:41:48.750
(electronic whooshing)

00:41:52.574 --> 00:41:54.851
- Decision making is a process, right?

00:41:54.851 --> 00:41:57.240
The question is, what
filters are you using

00:41:57.240 --> 00:41:58.420
to make decisions?

00:41:58.420 --> 00:42:00.863
Are you making decisions based
on the financial rewards,

00:42:00.863 --> 00:42:03.825
are you making decisions based
on how easy the work will be?

00:42:03.825 --> 00:42:05.819
I mean, I remember in college, you know,

00:42:05.819 --> 00:42:07.887
they would give you this
book, where all the students

00:42:07.887 --> 00:42:10.216
would rate the classes, and
they would rate things like

00:42:10.216 --> 00:42:12.206
how easy the class was, and how, you know,

00:42:12.206 --> 00:42:14.327
how much do they like professor.

00:42:14.327 --> 00:42:17.675
And you know, the first
year I picked all my classes

00:42:17.675 --> 00:42:21.179
based on workload, and I pick
everything a low workload.

00:42:21.179 --> 00:42:22.077
You know?

00:42:22.077 --> 00:42:27.052
And, pretty bored, didn't
work very hard, which is fine,

00:42:27.052 --> 00:42:30.686
but nothing was dynamic and
nothing really excited me.

00:42:30.686 --> 00:42:32.623
And I, thank goodness, learned that,

00:42:32.623 --> 00:42:34.418
and so the second year
I picked all my classes

00:42:34.418 --> 00:42:37.834
by professor rating,
regardless of the workload.

00:42:37.834 --> 00:42:40.176
So every class I had, I had these dynamic,

00:42:40.176 --> 00:42:43.033
amazing, incredible human beings
passing on their knowledge,

00:42:43.033 --> 00:42:45.626
and you were excited
to work hard for them.

00:42:45.626 --> 00:42:46.654
You know?

00:42:46.654 --> 00:42:49.087
And so again, the question is,

00:42:49.087 --> 00:42:50.511
what are the filters we're using?

00:42:50.511 --> 00:42:52.685
And so if you're only
chasing the mighty dollar,

00:42:52.685 --> 00:42:54.745
then you will have jobs that'll pay you

00:42:54.745 --> 00:42:57.452
a little more than the last,
but are you enjoying yourself?

00:42:57.452 --> 00:42:58.904
And I talked to a guy recently,

00:42:58.904 --> 00:43:00.577
who was in, he's in bad shape, like,

00:43:00.577 --> 00:43:03.263
he really hates his life,
and he's really depressed,

00:43:03.263 --> 00:43:04.561
and he doesn't know what to do.

00:43:04.561 --> 00:43:06.370
And so we were going
through all his old jobs,

00:43:06.370 --> 00:43:07.768
you know, and I said, give
me a job that you've loved.

00:43:07.768 --> 00:43:08.856
And he hadn't!

00:43:08.856 --> 00:43:10.952
Every single job he's
chosen out of college

00:43:10.952 --> 00:43:13.713
he picked because of the
money, and if something offered

00:43:13.713 --> 00:43:15.508
him more somewhere else, he took it.

00:43:15.508 --> 00:43:16.341
You know?

00:43:16.341 --> 00:43:17.828
Regardless.

00:43:17.828 --> 00:43:20.511
And the amazing thing is, he plateaued,

00:43:20.511 --> 00:43:22.490
because if you're only chasing the result,

00:43:22.490 --> 00:43:26.387
if you're only chasing the
thing that makes it easy, right?

00:43:26.387 --> 00:43:28.864
Then eventually you will get bored,

00:43:28.864 --> 00:43:30.856
or they'll get bored of you, right?

00:43:30.856 --> 00:43:32.215
And you plateau.

00:43:32.215 --> 00:43:33.342
In other words, chasing
the almighty dollar,

00:43:33.342 --> 00:43:35.917
if that's your only thing,
it eventually flattens out.

00:43:35.917 --> 00:43:38.305
Whereas if you're chasing
the thing that excites you,

00:43:38.305 --> 00:43:40.704
the human beings to be around,
the work that excites you,

00:43:40.704 --> 00:43:45.351
the stuff that you know, you
can get passionate about,

00:43:45.351 --> 00:43:46.184
you know?

00:43:46.184 --> 00:43:49.278
The irony is, is that you'll
actually make way, way more!

00:43:49.278 --> 00:43:50.111
Right?

00:43:50.111 --> 00:43:52.646
Because you're excited, and
they appreciate your excitement,

00:43:52.646 --> 00:43:53.553
and they reward your excitement,

00:43:53.553 --> 00:43:54.627
and you're better at your work

00:43:54.627 --> 00:43:56.915
because you want to work
harder, and all of that stuff,

00:43:56.915 --> 00:43:58.992
you don't have to strain to work harder.

00:43:58.992 --> 00:44:02.919
So decision making is simply
a matter of filters, you know?

00:44:02.919 --> 00:44:04.820
And so I've made decisions in my life

00:44:04.820 --> 00:44:06.472
that I would rather be happy than right,

00:44:06.472 --> 00:44:10.046
I'd rather do good than get rich,

00:44:10.046 --> 00:44:14.239
and so the decisions I
make put me in positions

00:44:14.239 --> 00:44:16.388
where when I leave any engagement,

00:44:16.388 --> 00:44:19.030
when I leave any meeting, I
feel that I've contributed.

00:44:19.030 --> 00:44:20.269
Right?

00:44:20.269 --> 00:44:23.232
Rare are the times anymore
where you walk away going,

00:44:23.232 --> 00:44:24.891
just think of the money,
just think of the money,

00:44:24.891 --> 00:44:26.331
think of the money, you know?

00:44:26.331 --> 00:44:28.287
'Cause that doesn't feel nice.

00:44:28.287 --> 00:44:31.625
And the experience I have, I
don't enjoy traveling to them,

00:44:31.625 --> 00:44:33.053
and I don't enjoy traveling home.

00:44:33.053 --> 00:44:34.380
Where if I have an amazing experience,

00:44:34.380 --> 00:44:35.623
I am looking forward to getting there

00:44:35.623 --> 00:44:36.456
and I'm excited when I leave!

00:44:36.456 --> 00:44:37.389
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:44:37.389 --> 00:44:40.035
- You know, so it's just decision making,

00:44:40.035 --> 00:44:42.211
decision making is just a
matter of what filters you use,

00:44:42.211 --> 00:44:45.066
and if you're good about keeping
those filters up and clear,

00:44:45.066 --> 00:44:46.775
then make your decision.

00:44:46.775 --> 00:44:48.932
I don't judge anybody by how,

00:44:48.932 --> 00:44:51.378
if they choose to use different filters,

00:44:51.378 --> 00:44:55.113
these are just the filters
I choose to live my life,

00:44:55.113 --> 00:44:55.946
you know?

00:44:55.946 --> 00:44:58.214
Not right or wrong, just
those are my decisions.

00:44:58.214 --> 00:44:59.621
You know, that's my filter.

00:44:59.621 --> 00:45:02.831
How can you help the human race?

00:45:02.831 --> 00:45:04.778
How can you help the human race,

00:45:04.778 --> 00:45:07.938
the human species, progress?

00:45:07.938 --> 00:45:09.612
I'm not joking, either,

00:45:09.612 --> 00:45:11.937
this is something we
all have to be aware of.

00:45:11.937 --> 00:45:14.621
At the end of the day, the
human animal is a social animal.

00:45:14.621 --> 00:45:17.378
And our very survival
depends on our ability

00:45:17.378 --> 00:45:19.954
to form communities, to form cultures.

00:45:19.954 --> 00:45:21.885
What's a community, what's a culture?

00:45:21.885 --> 00:45:23.347
It's a group of people with a common set

00:45:23.347 --> 00:45:25.062
of values and beliefs, right?

00:45:25.062 --> 00:45:25.895
What's a country?

00:45:25.895 --> 00:45:26.728
It's a group of people with a common set

00:45:26.728 --> 00:45:27.767
of values and beliefs.

00:45:27.767 --> 00:45:28.763
What's a company?

00:45:28.763 --> 00:45:30.238
It should be a group of people

00:45:30.238 --> 00:45:32.922
with a common set of values and beliefs.

00:45:32.922 --> 00:45:35.207
When we're surrounded by people
who believe what we believe,

00:45:35.207 --> 00:45:37.465
something remarkable happens.

00:45:37.465 --> 00:45:38.632
Trust emerges.

00:45:39.554 --> 00:45:41.745
And make no mistake of
it, trust is a feeling,

00:45:41.745 --> 00:45:43.656
and distinctly human feeling.

00:45:43.656 --> 00:45:46.702
You know, we all have friends
who are total screw ups,

00:45:46.702 --> 00:45:48.393
and yet we still trust them, right?

00:45:48.393 --> 00:45:49.810
(audience laughing)

00:45:49.810 --> 00:45:51.111
Trust is not a checklist,

00:45:51.111 --> 00:45:52.902
simply doing everything
you say you're going to do

00:45:52.902 --> 00:45:55.186
does not mean people will
inherently trust you,

00:45:55.186 --> 00:45:57.361
it just means you're reliable.

00:45:57.361 --> 00:45:58.528
We need trust.

00:45:59.870 --> 00:46:00.703
Right?

00:46:00.703 --> 00:46:01.753
We need trust.

00:46:01.753 --> 00:46:02.741
When we're surrounded by people

00:46:02.741 --> 00:46:05.263
who believe what we believe,
and trust starts emerging,

00:46:05.263 --> 00:46:06.892
when we trust them, and they trust us,

00:46:06.892 --> 00:46:08.651
we're more willing to take risks,

00:46:08.651 --> 00:46:11.271
we're more willing to experiment,

00:46:11.271 --> 00:46:14.838
which requires failure,
we're more willing to explore

00:46:14.838 --> 00:46:17.704
and go somewhere that no
one has ever gone before

00:46:17.704 --> 00:46:20.131
with the confidence that if we fail,

00:46:20.131 --> 00:46:22.313
if we trip over, if we turn our backs,

00:46:22.313 --> 00:46:23.905
that those within our community,

00:46:23.905 --> 00:46:26.044
those who we trust, and who trust us,

00:46:26.044 --> 00:46:27.740
will look after us while we're gone,

00:46:27.740 --> 00:46:29.316
will pick us up when we fall over,

00:46:29.316 --> 00:46:31.029
will help us when we're hurt.

00:46:31.029 --> 00:46:33.696
Our very survival depends on it.

00:46:35.175 --> 00:46:38.635
We're not good at everything,
we're not good by ourselves.

00:46:38.635 --> 00:46:40.733
You know, if I send you out to go

00:46:40.733 --> 00:46:42.342
fight a sabertooth tiger by yourself,

00:46:42.342 --> 00:46:44.378
odds are tiger one, you zero.

00:46:44.378 --> 00:46:45.587
(audience laughing)

00:46:45.587 --> 00:46:47.380
It's not going to go very well.

00:46:47.380 --> 00:46:51.079
But if you go out as a group,
we're pretty damn amazing.

00:46:51.079 --> 00:46:53.782
And the reason is, is because
we all have our own strengths,

00:46:53.782 --> 00:46:55.082
and we all have our certain weaknesses.

00:46:55.082 --> 00:46:57.378
And the goal is not to
fix your weaknesses,

00:46:57.378 --> 00:46:59.530
the goal is to amplify your strengths,

00:46:59.530 --> 00:47:01.062
and surround yourself with the people

00:47:01.062 --> 00:47:03.112
who can do what you can't do.

00:47:03.112 --> 00:47:05.793
But it's not just based on skills,

00:47:05.793 --> 00:47:08.535
and application, and experience,

00:47:08.535 --> 00:47:10.309
it's based on what you believe,

00:47:10.309 --> 00:47:11.935
it's based on what you believe.

00:47:11.935 --> 00:47:13.355
You see, simply being good at something,

00:47:13.355 --> 00:47:15.499
and having somebody else be
good at what you're no good at

00:47:15.499 --> 00:47:17.325
does not mean you will trust each other.

00:47:17.325 --> 00:47:20.065
Trust, the sense of trust,
comes from the sense of

00:47:20.065 --> 00:47:23.622
common values and common beliefs.

00:47:23.622 --> 00:47:24.852
And I'll prove it.

00:47:24.852 --> 00:47:26.794
How many of you are from New York?

00:47:26.794 --> 00:47:28.578
Okay, a bunch of you.

00:47:28.578 --> 00:47:31.058
Are you friends with
everybody in New York?

00:47:31.058 --> 00:47:32.260
(audience laughing)

00:47:32.260 --> 00:47:33.093
Why not?

00:47:33.093 --> 00:47:34.147
Why not?

00:47:34.147 --> 00:47:35.405
But when you go to Los Angeles,

00:47:35.405 --> 00:47:36.693
and you meet somebody from New York,

00:47:36.693 --> 00:47:37.780
you're like "Hey, I'm from New York!"

00:47:37.780 --> 00:47:39.664
And you're best friends.

00:47:39.664 --> 00:47:41.006
(audience laughing)
Right?

00:47:41.006 --> 00:47:42.649
And when you go to France, there you are

00:47:42.649 --> 00:47:44.574
on the Paris metro
minding your own business,

00:47:44.574 --> 00:47:46.898
and you hear an American
accent behind you,

00:47:46.898 --> 00:47:48.709
and you turn around, and you
say, "Hey, where you from?"

00:47:48.709 --> 00:47:49.996
They say, "Los Angeles."

00:47:49.996 --> 00:47:51.170
You're like, "Hey, I'm from New York!"

00:47:51.170 --> 00:47:52.142
And you're best friends.

00:47:52.142 --> 00:47:53.159
(audience laughing)

00:47:53.159 --> 00:47:54.993
Because you're surrounded by people

00:47:54.993 --> 00:47:56.612
who don't believe what you believe,

00:47:56.612 --> 00:47:58.199
when you're in a strange environment

00:47:58.199 --> 00:47:59.793
where you don't feel comfortable,

00:47:59.793 --> 00:48:01.718
you look for anyone who may share

00:48:01.718 --> 00:48:03.933
some of the same values
and beliefs that you have

00:48:03.933 --> 00:48:05.432
and you start to form a very real

00:48:05.432 --> 00:48:07.346
and very intense bond with them,

00:48:07.346 --> 00:48:08.942
simply because you know that they have

00:48:08.942 --> 00:48:11.296
a basic understanding of how you grew up,

00:48:11.296 --> 00:48:12.602
of the things that you care about,

00:48:12.602 --> 00:48:14.373
of the live that you lived back home.

00:48:14.373 --> 00:48:16.707
Well the same is true when we go to work.

00:48:16.707 --> 00:48:18.631
Do we want to go to work with people

00:48:18.631 --> 00:48:20.294
who understand us, who
believe what we believe,

00:48:20.294 --> 00:48:21.703
who have a similar view of the world

00:48:21.703 --> 00:48:22.761
that has nothing to do with their opinions

00:48:22.761 --> 00:48:24.278
and the differences that we share,

00:48:24.278 --> 00:48:26.070
that's good, that's called diversity,

00:48:26.070 --> 00:48:28.120
that's called advantages
to problem solving,

00:48:28.120 --> 00:48:29.828
which is we can all look at the same thing

00:48:29.828 --> 00:48:32.181
from a different angle and
come up with solutions.

00:48:32.181 --> 00:48:33.948
What I'm talking about is why should you

00:48:33.948 --> 00:48:35.536
help each other in the first place?

00:48:35.536 --> 00:48:37.600
What are you in pursuit of?

00:48:37.600 --> 00:48:39.670
Now the question is, is what creates

00:48:39.670 --> 00:48:41.306
that sense of values and beliefs?

00:48:41.306 --> 00:48:43.091
What creates that sense of trust?

00:48:43.091 --> 00:48:43.963
Right?

00:48:43.963 --> 00:48:45.686
Our very human instinct,
we know how to find

00:48:45.686 --> 00:48:47.906
people who believe what we believe,

00:48:47.906 --> 00:48:50.702
our survival depends on it.

00:48:50.702 --> 00:48:53.421
We're biologically gifted with this idea.

00:48:53.421 --> 00:48:55.695
If I ask you to go out in the street

00:48:55.695 --> 00:48:57.599
and find all the people who
believe what you believe,

00:48:57.599 --> 00:48:59.561
you know exactly what to do.

00:48:59.561 --> 00:49:01.567
You're going to strike up conversations,

00:49:01.567 --> 00:49:03.434
you're going to start talking to people,

00:49:03.434 --> 00:49:05.738
and either you'll have a
good feeling about them,

00:49:05.738 --> 00:49:06.823
or you won't.

00:49:06.823 --> 00:49:09.273
Either you'll have "chemistry",
whatever that means,

00:49:09.273 --> 00:49:11.044
or you don't.

00:49:11.044 --> 00:49:13.306
Sometimes it's quick, sometimes it's slow,

00:49:13.306 --> 00:49:14.592
but we know how to do it.

00:49:14.592 --> 00:49:16.670
It's called making friends,
it's called dating,

00:49:16.670 --> 00:49:17.825
it's called networking.

00:49:17.825 --> 00:49:20.101
We have the innate ability to do it.

00:49:20.101 --> 00:49:21.018
True story.

00:49:22.097 --> 00:49:25.090
There was a former Under
Secretary of Defense

00:49:25.090 --> 00:49:28.194
who was invited to give a
speech at a large conference,

00:49:28.194 --> 00:49:29.671
about a thousand people.

00:49:29.671 --> 00:49:33.240
And he was standing on the
stage with his cup of coffee,

00:49:33.240 --> 00:49:37.187
in a styrofoam cup, giving
his prepared remarks

00:49:37.187 --> 00:49:39.122
with his PowerPoint behind him.

00:49:39.122 --> 00:49:41.313
And he took a sip of his
coffee, and he smiled,

00:49:41.313 --> 00:49:43.497
and he looked down at the coffee,

00:49:43.497 --> 00:49:45.259
and then he went off script.

00:49:45.259 --> 00:49:47.853
And he said, "You know, last year I spoke

00:49:47.853 --> 00:49:50.078
"at this exact same conference.

00:49:50.078 --> 00:49:52.703
"Last year I was still
the Under Secretary.

00:49:52.703 --> 00:49:55.206
"And when I spoke here last year,

00:49:55.206 --> 00:49:57.382
"they flew me here business class,

00:49:57.382 --> 00:49:59.555
"and when I arrived at the airport

00:49:59.555 --> 00:50:02.657
"there was somebody waiting
for me to take me to my hotel.

00:50:02.657 --> 00:50:04.410
"And they took me to my hotel,

00:50:04.410 --> 00:50:05.941
"and they had already checked me in,

00:50:05.941 --> 00:50:07.798
"and they just took me up to my room.

00:50:07.798 --> 00:50:09.510
"And the next morning I came downstairs

00:50:09.510 --> 00:50:12.088
"and there was someone waiting
in the lobby to greet me,

00:50:12.088 --> 00:50:14.801
"and they drove me to
this here same venue.

00:50:14.801 --> 00:50:17.083
"They took me through the back entrance

00:50:17.083 --> 00:50:18.572
"and took me into the green room

00:50:18.572 --> 00:50:20.233
"and handed me a cup of coffee

00:50:20.233 --> 00:50:22.650
"in a beautiful ceramic cup."

00:50:24.217 --> 00:50:26.376
He says, "I'm no longer
the Under Secretary,

00:50:26.376 --> 00:50:28.257
"I flew here coach.

00:50:28.257 --> 00:50:31.403
"I took a taxi to my hotel
and I checked myself in.

00:50:31.403 --> 00:50:33.879
"When I came down to
the lobby this morning

00:50:33.879 --> 00:50:36.460
"I took another taxi to this venue.

00:50:36.460 --> 00:50:40.138
"I came in the front door
and found my way backstage,

00:50:40.138 --> 00:50:43.154
"and when I asked someone,
'Do you have any coffee?'

00:50:43.154 --> 00:50:45.415
"he pointed to the coffee
machine in the corner,

00:50:45.415 --> 00:50:47.986
"and I poured myself a cup of coffee

00:50:47.986 --> 00:50:51.084
"into this here styrofoam cup."

00:50:51.084 --> 00:50:54.596
He says, "The lesson is,
the ceramic cup was never

00:50:54.596 --> 00:50:59.068
"meant for me, it was meant
for the position I held.

00:50:59.068 --> 00:51:01.401
"I deserve a styrofoam cup."

00:51:02.283 --> 00:51:03.913
Remember this.

00:51:03.913 --> 00:51:07.768
As you gain fame, as you gain fortune,

00:51:07.768 --> 00:51:10.580
as you gain position and seniority,

00:51:10.580 --> 00:51:12.429
people will treat you better.

00:51:12.429 --> 00:51:14.285
They will hold doors open for you,

00:51:14.285 --> 00:51:15.840
they will get you a cup of tea

00:51:15.840 --> 00:51:17.885
and coffee without you even asking.

00:51:17.885 --> 00:51:19.840
They will call you "sir", and "ma'am",

00:51:19.840 --> 00:51:21.576
and they will give you stuff.

00:51:21.576 --> 00:51:23.851
None of that stuff is meant for you.

00:51:23.851 --> 00:51:27.023
That stuff is meant for
the position you hold.

00:51:27.023 --> 00:51:29.574
It is meant for the level
that you have achieved

00:51:29.574 --> 00:51:32.951
of leader, or success, or
whatever you want to call it.

00:51:32.951 --> 00:51:36.784
But you will always
deserve the styrofoam cup.

00:51:38.468 --> 00:51:42.809
Remember that, remember
that lesson of humility

00:51:42.809 --> 00:51:43.998
and gratitude.

00:51:43.998 --> 00:51:44.831
You can accept all the free stuff,

00:51:44.831 --> 00:51:49.621
you can accept all the perks,
absolutely you can enjoy them.

00:51:49.621 --> 00:51:51.141
But just be grateful for them,

00:51:51.141 --> 00:51:53.210
and know that they're not for you.

00:51:53.210 --> 00:51:55.960
I remember getting off the Acela,

00:51:56.924 --> 00:52:00.073
I took the Acela from New
York to Washington DC,

00:52:00.073 --> 00:52:02.846
and I got off the train
like everybody else,

00:52:02.846 --> 00:52:06.010
and I was walking down the
platform like everyone else,

00:52:06.010 --> 00:52:09.427
and I walked past General Norty Schwartz,

00:52:10.460 --> 00:52:13.307
who used to be the chief of staff

00:52:13.307 --> 00:52:17.090
of the United States Air Force,
the head of the Air Force.

00:52:17.090 --> 00:52:18.721
And here I did, you know, see a guy,

00:52:18.721 --> 00:52:22.291
in a suit, schlepping his own
suitcase down the platform

00:52:22.291 --> 00:52:25.578
just like me, and just a couple months ago

00:52:25.578 --> 00:52:28.207
he was flying on private
jets, and he had an entourage,

00:52:28.207 --> 00:52:30.730
and other people carried his luggage.

00:52:30.730 --> 00:52:32.409
But he no longer held the position,

00:52:32.409 --> 00:52:35.321
and so now he got to
drag his own suitcase.

00:52:35.321 --> 00:52:37.889
And never did it, sort of remind me more,

00:52:37.889 --> 00:52:40.411
that none of us deserve
the perks that we get,

00:52:40.411 --> 00:52:42.182
we all deserve a styrofoam cup.

